.TH "DOCUMENT.CREATEATTRIBUTENS" "3JS" "July 17, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Document.createAttributeNS \- Document: createAttributeNS() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]createAttributeNS()\f[B]\f[R] method of the
\f[CR]Document\f[R] interface creates a new attribute node with the
specified namespace URI and qualified name.
.PP
The object created is a node implementing the \f[CR]Attr\f[R] interface.
The DOM does not enforce what sort of attributes can be added to a
particular element in this manner.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
createAttributeNS(namespaceURI, qualifiedName)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]namespaceURI\f[R]
A string that specifies the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] to associate with
the attribute, or the empty string.
In HTML documents, most attributes are in the \f[B]null namespace\f[R]
\(em use the empty string for these.
Use a specific namespace URI only when creating a namespaced attribute,
such as \f[CR]xml:lang\f[R] or \f[CR]xml:space\f[R].
Some namespace URIs are:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
XML: \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] (for
\f[CR]xml:lang\f[R], \f[CR]xml:space\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
XMLNS: \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R] (for \f[CR]xmlns\f[R],
\f[CR]xmlns:*\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
XLink: \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\f[R] (for
\f[CR]xlink:href\f[R], \f[CR]xlink:title\f[R], etc.)
.RE
.TP
\f[B]qualifiedName\f[R]
A string containing the qualified name of the new attribute.
The \f[CR]name\f[R] property of the created attribute is initialized
with this value.
.RS
.PP
The format of the qualified name is \f[CR]prefix:localName\f[R] or
\f[CR]localName\f[R], where the parts are defined as:
.TP
\f[B]prefix\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
A \(lqshort alias\(rq for the namespace.
The prefix is optional, but if it is specified the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] parameter must also be specified.
If the prefix is set to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], the
\f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] must be set to
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R], respectively.
.RS
.PP
The value is used to initialize the new attribute\(cqs \f[CR]prefix\f[R]
property.
Defaults to \f[CR]null\f[R].
.RE
.TP
\f[B]localName\f[R]
The local name of the attribute.
The value is used to initialize the new attribute\(cqs
\f[CR]localName\f[R] property.
.RE
.SS Return value
The new \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]NamespaceError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] value is:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
not a valid namespace URI.
.IP \(bu 2
set to the empty string when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] has a value.
.IP \(bu 2
not the value \f[CR]http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\f[R] or
\f[CR]http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/\f[R] when \f[CR]prefix\f[R] is set
to \f[CR]xml\f[R] or \f[CR]xmlns\f[R], respectively.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]InvalidCharacterError\f[R] \f[B]DOMException\f[R]
Thrown if either the \f[CR]prefix\f[R] or \f[CR]localName\f[R] is not
valid:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]prefix\f[R] must have at least one character, and cannot
contain ASCII whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], or \f[CR]>\f[R]
(U+0000, U+002F, or U+003E, respectively).
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]localName\f[R] must have at least one character, and may not
contain ASCII whitespace, \f[CR]NULL\f[R], \f[CR]/\f[R], \f[CR]=\f[R] or
\f[CR]>\f[R] (U+0000, U+002F, U+003D, or U+003E, respectively).
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Earlier versions of the specification were more
restrictive, requiring that the \f[CR]localName\f[R] be a valid \c
.UR https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#dt-name
XML name
.UE \c
\&.
.RE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Creating a namespaced attribute
This example creates an \f[CR]xml:lang\f[R] attribute with the XML
namespace and attaches it to a paragraph element.
This attribute specifies the language of the element\(cqs content for
XML processing.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]p\f[R] id=\(dqgreeting\(dq>Bonjour!</\f[B]p\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] el = document.getElementById(\(dqgreeting\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] attr = document.createAttributeNS(
  \(dqhttp://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace\(dq,
  \(dqxml:lang\(dq,
);
attr.value = \(dqfr\(dq;
el.setAttributeNode(attr);
.EE
.SS Creating an unprefixed attribute
In HTML documents, unprefixed attributes (such as SVG presentation
attributes like \f[CR]viewBox\f[R]) are in the null namespace.
Use the empty string for the \f[CR]namespaceURI\f[R] parameter to match
this.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]svg\f[R] id=\(dqsvg\(dq></\f[B]svg\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] svg = document.getElementById(\(dqsvg\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] attr = document.createAttributeNS(\(dq\(dq, \(dqviewBox\(dq);
attr.value = \(dq0 0 100 100\(dq;
svg.setAttributeNode(attr);
console.log(svg.getAttribute(\(dqviewBox\(dq)); \f[I]// \(dq0 0 100 100\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Note that, in most cases, you can use \f[CR]Element.setAttribute()\f[R]
instead of \f[CR]createAttributeNS()\f[R] for unprefixed attributes:
.IP
.EX
svg.setAttribute(\(dqviewBox\(dq, \(dq0 0 100 100\(dq);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
